<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Events' cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because one of
    its consumers is still in Base: SearchService lives in Humans.Application, so a
    Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Events would make Base reference a section and
    cycle (memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    ZERO ProjectReference, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING — DO NOT ADD ONE. The surface is
    IEventServiceRead plus two NodaTime-only DTOs, so nothing here needs a Humans project.
    Humans.Email.Contracts references THIS project (EventStatus, a constructor parameter and
    switch subject of EventLifecycleNotification), and that leaf-to-leaf edge is only safe
    because the chain terminates here. Adding a ProjectReference — Humans.Interfaces for a
    marker interface is the likely first attempt — extends the chain past its terminator and
    puts the whole end-state graph's acyclicity at the mercy of what Base later references.
    The build will not stop you: Humans.Interfaces has no project references of its own yet.
    G5 lane 3b, nobodies-collective/Humans#866.

    If a type here ever genuinely needs something from another project, move the type out
    instead of adding the reference.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
